I'll implement the satellite tree reconstruction algorithm.

The approach:
1. The first element in pre-order is always the root
2. Find that element in the in-order array - everything to its left is the left subtree, everything to its right is the right subtree
3. Recursively build the left and right subtrees

Satellite.java
```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Satellite {
    public Tree treeFromTraversals(List<Character> preorderInput, List<Character> inorderInput) {
        if (preorderInput.size() != inorderInput.size()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("traversals must have the same length");
        }
        
        if (preorderInput.size() != new ArrayList<Character>(preorderInput).size()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("traversals must contain unique elements");
        }
        
        // Check for unique elements
        List<Character> preorderCopy = new ArrayList<Character>(preorderInput);
        java.util.Collections.sort(preorderCopy);
        if (!preorderCopy.equals(new ArrayList<Character>(preorderInput))) {
            // Check uniqueness
            for (int i = 0; i < preorderInput.size(); i++) {
                for (int j = i + 1; j < preorderInput.size(); j++) {
                    if (preorderInput.get(i).equals(preorderInput.get(j))) {
                        throw new IllegalArgumentException("traversals must contain unique elements");
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        
        if (inorderInput.size() != new ArrayList<Character>(inorderInput).size()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("traversals must contain unique elements");
        }
        
        return buildTree(preorderInput, 0, preorderInput.size() - 1, inorderInput, 0, inorderInput.size() - 1);
    }
    
    private Tree buildTree(List<Character> preorder, int preStart, int preEnd, List<Character> inorder, int inStart, int inEnd) {
        if (preStart > preEnd || inStart > inEnd) {
            return null;
        }
        
        Character rootChar = preorder.get(preStart);
        Tree root = new Tree(rootChar);
        
        int rootIndexInInorder = inorder.indexOf(rootChar);
        
        int leftSubtreeSize = rootIndexInInorder - inStart;
        
        root.left = buildTree(preorder, preStart + 1, preStart + leftSubtreeSize, inorder, inStart, rootIndexInInorder - 1);
        root.right = buildTree(preorder, preStart + leftSubtreeSize + 1, preEnd, inorder, rootIndexInInorder + 1, inEnd);
        
        return root;
    }
}
```

Wait, I need to check the `Tree` class structure. Let me reconsider—the `Tree` class likely has `value`, `left`, and `right` fields. Let me provide a cleaner implementation:

