I'll implement the `StateOfTicTacToe` class to determine the game state of a tic-tac-toe game. Here's my approach:

1. First, I'll check if the board is valid by examining the counts of X's and O's and the players.
2. Then, I'll determine the game state based on the counts and winning positions.

Let me analyze the requirements:
- X starts the game, so there can be equal numbers of X's and O's (O just moved) or one more X than O's (X just moved)
- A game ends when someone wins or the board is full
- If both players have winning lines, the board is invalid
- If a player has won, the game should have stopped, so we can't have extra moves after a win

Here's my implementation:

StateOfTicTacToe.java
```java
class StateOfTicTacToe {
    public GameState determineState(String[] board) {
        int xCount = 0;
        int oCount = 0;
        
        // Count X's and O's
        for (char c : board[0].toCharArray()) {
            if (c == 'X') xCount++;
            else if (c == 'O') oCount++;
        }
        for (char c : board[1].toCharArray()) {
            if (c == 'X') xCount++;
            else if (c == 'O') oCount++;
        }
        for (char c : board[2].toCharArray()) {
            if (c == 'X') xCount++;
            else if (c == 'O') oCount++;
        }
        
        // Check for invalid turn order
        if (xCount < oCount || xCount > oCount + 1) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid turn order");
        }
        
        boolean xWins = hasWon(board, 'X');
        boolean oWins = hasWon(board, 'O');
        
        // Check for invalid winning states
        if (xWins && oWins) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Both players cannot win");
        }
        
        if (xWins) {
            // X has won, so O cannot have one more than X
            if (oCount > xCount) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid state after X wins");
            }
            return GameState.WIN;
        }
        
        if (oWins) {
            // O has won, so X and O counts must be equal
            if (xCount > oCount) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid state after O wins");
            }
            return GameState.WIN;
        }
        
        // Check for draw or ongoing game
        if (xCount + oCount == 9) {
            return GameState.DRAW;
        }
        
        return GameState.ONGOING;
    }
    
    private boolean hasWon(String[] board, char player) {
        // Check rows
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            if (board[i].charAt(0) == player && board[i].charAt(1) == player && board[i].charAt(2) == player) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        
        // Check columns
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            if (board[0].charAt(i) == player && board[1].charAt(i) == player && board[2].charAt(i) == player) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        
        // Check diagonals
        if (board[0].charAt(0) == player && board[1].charAt(1) == player && board[2].charAt(2) == player) {
            return true;
        }
        if (board[0].charAt(2) == player && board[1].charAt(1) == player && board[2].charAt(0) == player) {
            return true;
        }
        
        return false;
    }
}
```

This implementation:
1. Counts the number of X's and O's on the board
2. Validates the turn order (X starts, so X count is either equal to or one more than O count)
3. Checks if either player has won
4. If both players have won, the board is invalid
5. If X has won, it returns WIN and validates that O doesn't have more marks than X
6. If O has won, it returns WIN and validates that X doesn't have more marks than O
7. If no one has won, it checks if the board is full (draw) or if the game is ongoing

The logic follows the game rules: X starts, so the counts must be balanced, and a game can't continue after a win.
