I'll analyze the Pig Latin translation task and implement the four rules.

The key challenges are:
1. Detecting vowel starts (including "xr" and "yt" prefixes)
2. Handling consonant clusters
3. Special "qu" handling
4. Treating "y" specially when it follows consonants

Here's my implementation:

lib.rs
```rust
pub fn translate(input: &str) -> String {
    input
        .split_whitespace()
        .map(|word| translate_word(word))
        .collect::<Vec<_>>()
        .join(" ")
}

fn translate_word(word: &str) -> String {
    let bytes: Vec<char> = word.chars().collect();
    let len = bytes.len();
    
    // Rule 1: starts with vowel or "xr"/"yt"
    if is_vowel(bytes[0]) || starts_with_xr_or_yt(&bytes) {
        return format!("{}ay", word);
    }
    
    // Rule 3: "qu" handling
    if let Some(qu_pos) = find_qu_position(&bytes) {
        let prefix: String = bytes[..qu_pos].iter().collect();
        let rest: String = bytes[qu_pos..].iter().collect();
        return format!("{}{}ay", rest, prefix);
    }
    
    // Rule 2 or 4: consonants (possibly followed by 'y')
    let mut consonant_end = 0;
    for (i, &c) in bytes.iter().enumerate() {
        if c == 'y' {
            consonant_end = i;
            break;
        }
        if !is_vowel(c) {
            consonant_end = i + 1;
        } else {
            break;
        }
    }
    
    let prefix: String = bytes[..consonant_end].iter().collect();
    let rest: String = bytes[consonant_end..].iter().collect();
    format!("{}{}ay", rest, prefix)
}

fn is_vowel(c: char) -> bool {
    matches!(c, 'a' | 'e' | 'i' | 'o' | 'u')
}

fn starts_with_xr_or_yt(bytes: &[char]) -> bool {
    if bytes.len() < 2 {
        return false;
    }
    (bytes[0] == 'x' && bytes[1] == 'r') || (bytes[0] == 'y' && bytes[1] == 't')
}

fn find_qu_position(bytes: &[char]) -> Option<usize> {
    for i in 0..bytes.len().saturating_sub(1) {
        if bytes[i] == 'q' && bytes[i + 1] == 'u' {
            return Some(i);
        }
    }
    None
}
```
