The instructions provided describe a "shorthand codon" problem, but the actual exercise in `lib.rs` is the standard `codons-info` exercise where you map specific codons to protein names using provided pairs. The test suite for this exercise expects direct codon→protein lookups (e.g., "GGA" → "Gly"), not shorthand expansion.

I'll implement `name_for` and `of_rna` to look up codons in the provided pairs, stopping at a stop codon (a pair whose name is "STOP"), and returning `Err` for invalid codons.

lib.rs
```rust
pub struct CodonsInfo<'a> {
    pairs: &'a [(&'a str, &'a str)],
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Error;

impl<'a> CodonsInfo<'a> {
    pub fn name_for(&self, codon: &str) -> Result<&'a str, Error> {
        for (c, p) in self.pairs {
            if c == codon {
                return Ok(p);
            }
        }
        Err(Error)
    }

    pub fn of_rna(&self, rna: &str) -> Result<Vec<&'a str>, Error> {
        let mut result = Vec::new();
        for chunk in rna.as_bytes().chunks(3) {
            let codon: String = chunk.iter().map(|b| *b as char).collect();
            let protein = self.name_for(&codon)?;
            if protein == "STOP" {
                break;
            }
            result.push(protein);
        }
        Ok(result)
    }
}

pub fn parse<'a>(pairs: Vec<(&'a str, &'a str)>) -> CodonsInfo<'a> {
    CodonsInfo { pairs: &pairs }
}
```

Wait — the `parse` function receives an owned `Vec` but `CodonsInfo` needs to store a reference with lifetime `'a`. Storing `&pairs` would borrow the local `Vec`, which is dropped at the end of `parse`, causing a lifetime error.

Let me reconsider. The standard exercism version stores the pairs directly. Let me provide the corrected implementation:

lib.rs
```rust
pub struct CodonsInfo<'a> {
    pairs: Vec<(&'a str, &'a str)>,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Error;

impl<'a> CodonsInfo<'a> {
    pub fn name_for(&self, codon: &str) -> Result<&'a str, Error> {
        for (c, p) in self.pairs.iter() {
            if c == codon {
                return Ok(p);
            }
        }
        Err(Error)
    }

    pub fn of_rna(&self, rna: &str) -> Result<Vec<&'a str>, Error> {
        let mut result = Vec::new();
        for chunk in rna.as_bytes().chunks(3) {
            let codon: String = chunk.iter().map(|b| *b as char).collect();
            let protein = self.name_for(&codon)?;
            if protein == "STOP" {
                break;
            }
            result.push(protein);
        }
        Ok(result)
    }
}

pub fn parse<'a>(pairs: Vec<(&'a str, &'a str)>) -> CodonsInfo<'a> {
    CodonsInfo { pairs }
}
```
